During a transaction, EF Core reads the new timestamp using UPDATE  … + SELECT [timestamp]…

However, the read timestamp is not used.

If this is by design there should be at least a documentation telling the users that in transaction the timestamps are not updated.

When removing the transaction, the code works. While the transaction is not necessary in this simple case described below, it is necessary in other cases involving more and other objects.

The workaround we are using is updating the timestamp manually.

### Steps to reproduce

- A context with a simple class with a timestamp column:  .IsConcurrencyToken().ValueGeneratedOnAddOrUpdate()
- I start a transaction: ctx.Database.BeginTransaction()
- I change two objects 
- Object 2 contains too much letters in a string
- SaveChanges() fails (DbUpdateException, which is OK of course, as we have too much letters)
- I reduce the letters in the string to fit the max string length.
- SaveChanges() fails again with a DbUpdateConcurrencyException for object 1 
- The DbUpdateConcurrencyException is not ok as there was definitely no concurrent access to the database!
- Same problem with every subsequent call of SaveChanges()
- Changing the isolation level of the transaction makes no difference. 

The reason for DbUpdateConcurrencyException can be seen in the following screenshot:

- In the first try of SaveChanges() there is an UPDATE followed by a SELECT [Timestamp]… for object 1, see green arrow
- However, the new timestamp  (…808) is not copied to the entity object in RAM. This object still continues to use the old timestamp (…806), see red arrow. The screenshot proves that EF Core can read the correct timestamp using ctx.Entry(p).GetDatabaseValues().GetValue<byte[]>("Timestamp"), but not uses it.
- With this wrong timestamp all subsequent tries to persist the object cannot succeed.

